From a265e734459ef4cf2762fd30e6467c5b967603d7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tomaszstaniak <68373859+tomaszstaniak@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 18:20:27 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] posixc: keep flock() locks per open file description

flock() implemented a lock as a public exec SignalSemaphore named
FLOCK(path). Semaphores belong to a task, so:

- a second open() of a locked file in the same task got the lock again,
  because the semaphore nests for its owner;
- LOCK_UN released the semaphore whatever the descriptor held, so
  unlocking a descriptor without a lock removed another descriptor's lock;
- nothing released a lock when its descriptor was closed or the program
  ended, so the file stayed locked until posixc.library was expunged.

Locks now belong to the open file description (fcb), as in BSD and
POSIX: a registry in the library base lists the locked files with the
descriptions holding a lock and the requests waiting for one. close()
and closedir() release the lock when the last descriptor of a
description goes, which also covers program exit (__exit_fd closes all
descriptors). LOCK_UN only removes the lock of the given description. A
conflicting request from another description fails with EWOULDBLOCK
under LOCK_NB and waits otherwise, on a private signal bit; when every
conflicting lock was obtained by the calling task, a blocking request
fails with EDEADLK instead of hanging the task for good. Conversions
between shared and exclusive keep BSD behaviour.

The flock test opened its file with O_CREAT alone, which posixc rejects
with EINVAL, so it never reached flock(); it now opens the file
O_RDWR | O_CREAT. A new test, flock_description, covers the three cases
above, shared locks of two descriptions and conversions.
